{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'For transparent localization|operational ROI' report.md\"","aggregated_output":"19:The original NARPS instructions specify the directional hypothesis as a positive parametric gain effect in vmPFC for the equal-indifference group and ask teams to report whole-brain-corrected results ([instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf)). vmPFC does not have one universally agreed anatomical boundary; the NARPS report describes using a conjunction of anatomical and meta-analytic information to operationalize it ([NARPS study](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2314-9)). Here, the primary support criterion is a positive whole-brain-corrected voxel in an independently specified vmPFC mask. For transparent localization, the mask uses Harvard–Oxford cortical atlas labels for Frontal Medial and Subcallosal Cortex, plus the Frontal Orbital label restricted to |MNI x| ≤ 20 mm and z ≤ 30 mm. It is resampled to the participant-map grid using nearest-neighbour interpolation and intersected with common image coverage. This is an operational ROI, not a uniquely established boundary.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
